    "case_id": "555",
    "case_summary": "A 74-year-old woman with hypertension, diabetes mellitus, and chronic kidney disease presented with severe exertional dyspnea. She underwent aortic and mitral valve–replacement surgery. On postoperative day 5, she experienced a bradycardic episode leading to cardiac arrest requiring resuscitation and intubation. She developed progressive acute worsening of renal function complicated by pulmonary edema and hyperkalemia refractory to medical therapy (peak creatinine, 4.42; baseline, 1.5). The patient had not received adequate enteral feeding for 48 hours.\n\nInitial physical examination findings were not explicitly stated in the case report.\n\nLaboratory Tests:\n- Serum β-hydroxybutyrate elevated to 1.81 mmol/L from 0.7 mmol/L\n- Blood glucose level between 130 and 160 mmol/L\n- Normal lactate level\n- Normalized blood urea nitrogen\n- Persistent anion-gap metabolic acidosis\n\nImaging Studies:\n- Transesophageal echocardiogram\n  Title: Initial Cardiac Assessment\n  Description: Showed severe mitral regurgitation and moderate aortic stenosis (mean gradient: 23 mm Hg)\n\nDetailed Laboratory Values (Table 1):\n- Sodium: 131-137 mmol/L\n- Potassium: 4.1-5.2 mmol/L\n- Chloride: 97-103 mmol/L\n- Bicarbonate: 21-24 mmol/L\n- Creatinine: 1.55-4.42 mg/dL\n- Glucose: 128-142 mg/dL\n- Arterial pH: 7.26-7.40\n- β-Hydroxybutyrate: 0.24-1.81 mmol/L",
